course: biology
lesson: 147
title: Spider-Man: real spider silk and webs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Spider-Man shoots webs from his wrists, swings between skyscrapers on a single thread and stops a runaway train with a giant web.
explain: Real spiders make silk in glands inside the abdomen and pull it out through little nozzles called spinnerets at the back end; it starts as a liquid and turns into a thread as it is stretched.
explain: Weight for weight, some spider silk is stronger than steel and far stretchier, so it soaks up the crash of a flying insect without snapping, but a real thread is far too thin to hold a person.
explain: On an autumn morning, look at a hedge: dew sparkles on dozens of round webs you never noticed, each with a garden spider waiting in the middle, feeling for the shiver of a trapped fly.
fact: Spiders make silk in glands in the abdomen and pull it out through spinnerets at the back end, not from wrists or legs.
fact: A spider can make several kinds of silk: sticky silk for catching, strong dry silk for the web's frame and soft silk for wrapping eggs.
fact: Orb weavers build a fresh round web, often every day or night, with a sticky spiral across strong spokes, and many eat the old web to recycle the silk.
fact: Weight for weight, some spider silk is stronger than steel and far stretchier; Darwin's bark spider spins webs across rivers up to about 25 metres wide.
fact: Many young spiders travel by ballooning: they let out threads from a high point and float away on the air, sometimes far out to sea.
fact: Many spiders wrap trapped prey in silk to stop it struggling and keep it for later; spiders cannot chew, so they pour digestive juice on their prey and suck up the liquid.
fact: A spider walks on the dry spokes of its web and touches sticky threads carefully with special claws, but it can stick in a stranger's web.
fact: Spiders trail a safety line called a dragline, drop on it and climb back up; dragline silk is the strongest spider silk.
fact: Not all spiders build round webs: some build funnels, sheets or tangles, and some build none at all.
fact: A spider has eight legs; an insect, such as a fly, has six.
fact: A golden cape was woven from the silk of over a million golden orb weaver spiders in Madagascar; spiders cannot be farmed like silkworms because they eat each other.
fact: Real silk of Spider-Man's thickness could not stop a train, but silk is very good at soaking up energy by stretching.
fact: Dew collects on the cool threads at night and shows how many webs there are, often thousands in a garden in autumn.
exercise: Where does a real spider's silk come out? | From spinnerets at the back of its body | From its wrists, like Spider-Man | From its mouth, like a silkworm | From the tips of its legs
exercise: How does liquid silk become a thread? | It turns solid as the spider pulls and stretches it | It dries in the sun for an hour | It freezes in the cold night air | It mixes with drops of water from the dew and sets like jelly
exercise: What is the sticky spiral of a round web for? | Catching flying insects | Holding the web up in the wind | Wrapping the spider's eggs | Keeping the rain off the spider
exercise: How does the spider in the middle know something is trapped? | It feels the vibrations in the threads | It hears the insect crying for help | It sees every thread move from far off | It waits for the dew to shake
exercise: Weight for weight, how strong is some spider silk? | Stronger than steel and far stretchier | Weaker than wet paper | About as strong as a cotton thread of the same thickness, no more | As strong as a car tyre
exercise: Could a real spider thread hold Spider-Man up between skyscrapers? | No, a real thread is far too thin | Yes, a single thread holds a person easily | Yes, if the spider is big enough | No, silk breaks as soon as it gets wet
exercise: How do many young spiders travel far away? | Ballooning on silk threads carried by the air | Riding on the backs of birds that fly over the sea and the hills | Walking for many years | Floating on leaves down rivers
exercise: Why does a spider wrap a fly in silk? | To stop it struggling and keep it for later | To keep the fly warm for winter | To make a nest out of the fly | To hide the fly from its own babies
exercise: How does a spider eat, since it cannot chew? | It pours juice on its prey and sucks up the liquid | It swallows the insect whole | It grinds the insect up with hard teeth hidden inside its stomach | It nibbles it with tiny teeth
exercise: Why doesn't a spider stick to its own web? | It walks on the dry spokes and uses special claws | Its whole web has no sticky parts | It is too light to stick to anything | It covers the whole web with a coat of oil every single morning
exercise: What is a dragline? | A safety line the spider trails and drops on | The sticky spiral of a round web | A line the spider uses to drag its food back to the middle of the web | A thread used only for eggs
exercise: Do all spiders build round webs? | No, some build funnels, sheets, tangles or none | Yes, every spider builds a round web | No, only spiders that live in sheds and garages ever build webs | Yes, but some webs are square
exercise: Why can't spiders be farmed for silk like silkworms? | They fight and eat each other | Their silk is too sticky to use | They make silk only in the jungle | They stop making silk in a cage
exercise: How was the golden spider-silk cape made? | From the silk of over a million golden orb weavers | From the silk of one giant spider | From silkworms painted gold | From shiny plastic that was made in a factory to look like silk
exercise: Why is silk good at catching a fast insect without snapping? | It stretches, spreading the force over time | It is as hard as glass | It melts the insect on contact | It pulls the insect back towards the spider like a magnet does
exercise: Why do webs sparkle in a hedge on a cool morning? | Dew forms on the cool threads overnight | The spiders paint them with shiny silk | Sunlight makes the silk glow by itself | The webs are made of tiny crystals
test: «3 spiders in the bath, how many legs is that, shut up and count» | 24 | 18 | 12 | 32
test: «spider ate 2 flies, how many legs were in that web before lunch, spider too» | 20 | 16 | 24 | 14
test: In a cartoon, a spider shoots silk out of its mouth. What is real? | Silk comes out of spinnerets at the back end | Silk comes out of the mouth, just like that | Silk comes out of all eight legs | Silk comes out of the eyes
test: «tiny spider just flew past my face on a string, they can fly??» What is happening? | It is ballooning: a silk thread caught the air and carried it | Spiders have small hidden wings | It was thrown by a bigger spider | The wind blew its old web away and the spider was still holding on
test: What would happen if a spider fell into the sticky part of a stranger's web? | It could get stuck just like a fly | Nothing, spiders never stick to any web | It would eat the web at once | The web would let it go straight away
test: «Spider-Man stopped a whole train with webs, real silk could do that» What is right? | Silk of that thickness could not, though silk is very good at soaking up force | Yes, any spider web you find in a garden could stop a moving train | Yes, one real thread could stop a train | No, silk cannot stretch at all
test: The garden spider's web is gone in the morning and a new one is up by evening. Where did the old silk go? | Many orb weavers eat the old web and reuse the silk | The wind always blows it away to the next garden, where another spider uses it | Birds take every web for their nests | The rain melts it into the soil
test: «why's there a web like a tube in the corner of the shed, broken spider?» What is right? | Some spiders build funnel webs to catch walking insects | The spider forgot how to build a round web | It is just an ordinary round web that the wind has rolled up into a tube | Only sick spiders build funnels
test: Darwin's bark spider spins a web across a river. About how wide can it be? | Up to about 25 metres | About 25 centimetres | About 2 kilometres | Exactly 1 metre
test: «spider dropped off the ceiling right onto my desk, how'd it get back up» How? | It climbed up its dragline, the safety thread it let out | It jumped all the way up | It flew back up to the ceiling with a pair of small hidden wings | It walked up the air
test: «there's like 3 webs in the hedge, that's it» A frosty morning shows dozens. Why didn't you see them before? | Thin threads are hard to see until dew or frost covers them | The spiders built them all overnight | The frost made the webs grow | Webs only exist when it is cold
exam: Identify the organs from which a spider extrudes its silk. | The spinnerets situated at the posterior of the abdomen | Glands situated within the wrists of the forelimbs | The mouthparts, in the manner of the silkworm | The terminal segments of each of the eight legs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the strength of spider silk. | Weight for weight, certain silks exceed steel in strength and greatly exceed it in elasticity | Spider silk is weaker than wet paper of equal weight | A single natural thread is capable of supporting the full weight of an adult human | Spider silk loses all strength upon contact with water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which spiders consume their prey? | Being unable to chew, they apply digestive fluid and ingest the resulting liquid | They swallow their prey whole and entire | They masticate their prey thoroughly with numerous small teeth situated in the jaws | They store their prey indefinitely without consumption
exam: Ascertain the reason for which spiders have not been farmed for silk in the manner of silkworms. | Spiders kept together fight with and consume one another | Spider silk is too adhesive for any use | Spiders cease the production of silk altogether when kept in daylight or in captivity | Spider silk dissolves upon exposure to air
